Always peeled out of the egg: Max Raabe turns 59
He is able to inspire his audience with reinterpretations of hitters from the 20s and 30s.
He is currently on the road with the program "Good day, dear happiness" - if he is allowed to perform.
Max Raabe actually wanted to celebrate his birthday on stage in Leipzig this Sunday.
But that doesn't happen.
The planned concert has been moved to May 2022 - "on official instructions" to contain the corona virus.
Other performances planned during the Advent season must also be cancelled, as it says on the website of the singer, who turns 59 today, and his musicians (www.palast-orchester.de).
From January, the artists want to make up for their dates, which had already been postponed in 2020 due to the pandemic.
Born on December 12, 1962 in Lünen in North Rhine-Westphalia, Max Raabe liked to sing as a teenager.
In Berlin he studied opera singing, in 1986 he founded the Palast Orchester together with fellow students.
Since then, the baritone has established itself with its repertoire of hitters and chansons in the style of comedian harmonists.
He has received numerous awards.
His live album from the "MTV Unplugged" series (2019) - just like his successful album "Für Frauen ist nicht Problem" (2013) - reached the second place in the charts.
He's made it out of niche into the mainstream.
Always dressed elegantly, the blonde hair strictly combed back and thought on etiquette, Max Raabe is an indispensable part of the current German-speaking musical landscape.
With the program "Good day, dear happiness" he has been on tour since 2020. dpa
