Amazon founder Bezo sends astronaut daughter and moderator into space
The daughter of the first American in space, a TV presenter and four other passengers have taken a short trip into space.
They were transported on Saturday by Amazon founder Jeff Bezos "New Shepard's "New Shepard" rocket system.
Take-off and landing of the approximately ten-minute flight took place in the west of the US state of Texas.
Under a bright blue sky, Bezos personally received the newly crowned astronauts upon their return.
"Welcome back," Bezos said, dressed in a blue space suit as he opened the hatch to the capsule.
Under applause from friends, family members and Blue Origin employees, the six passengers gradually climbed out and embraced the Amazon founder.
According to Blue Origin, they were on the road for ten minutes and 13 seconds, reaching an altitude of 107 kilometers.
74-year-old Laura Shepard Churchley, daughter of Alan Shepard (1923-1998) flown into space as Nasa astronaut (1923-1998), after whom Blue Origin's missile system is named, and 50-year-old TV presenter Michael Strahan were guests of honour at the company, the other four passengers had paided for their tickets: space engineer Dylan Taylor, investor Evan Dick, technology entrepreneur Lane Bess as well as his son.
How much they each paid for their ticket, Blue Origin did not share.
Laura Shepard Churchley recalled her father's mission after the short flight.
He had to work hard in his assignments and do everything himself.
She just flew along, the 74-year-old said with a laughter.
Michael Strahan also expressed enthusiasm.
He would like to return to space right away, said the moderator.
"New Shepard" flies up to 100 kilometers altitude
The "New Shepard" missile system flies largely automatically.
The capsule separated from the rocket during the flight reached an altitude of about 100 kilometers above the earth, at times with weightlessness, before the reusable object landed down again from large parachutes.
The International Aviation Federation and many other experts see 100 kilometers above the Earth as a frontier to space, but there is no binding international regulation.
It was already Blue Origin's third manned all-excursion: At the first in July, Amazon founder Bezos himself was on board, along with his brother Mark, an 82-year-old former U.S. pilot and an 18-year-old Dutchman.
At the second in October, 90-year-old Canadian actor William Shatner, who became world-famous with his role as "Captain Kirk" in "Star Trek," former Nasa engineer Chris Boshuizen, entrepreneur Glen de Vries and Blue Origin's deputy chief executive, Audrey Powers.
Critics accuse Bezos and Blue Origin of advancing the business of space tourism largely without scientific research interests for the good of humanity and without regard for the climate.
