GDR handivess model Altbau E-Lok E 95 03 DRG/ DR, the locomotive was in use in Silesia and Central Germany, the very rare locomotive probably comes from the conversion proposal of the model railroader ME Heft 11/1968, used with game traces and missing parts see photos, function good, The series E 95 is a heavy freight train electric locomotive of the Deutsche Reichsbahn-Gesellschaft (DRG),
A locomotive is located in the DB Museum Halle (Saale) and we are operationally refurbished
In 1924, the Deutsche Reichsbahn-Gesellschaft instructed AEG to submit proposals for an electric locomotive, which should be able to transport 2,200-tonne coal trains and 530-tonne passenger trains with 45 km/h cruising speed on this route.
65 km/h were required as a top speed.
Thus, in March 1926, the DRG determined the delivery as 1’Co+Co1’ locomotive with Tatzlager drive AEG with the supply of the mechanical part, the Siemens Schuckertwerke with the electrical part.
From December 1927 to June 1928, six locomotives were supplied, which received the operating numbers E 95 01–06.
All survived the Second World War and then found themselves on the territory of the later GDR.
In 1946, the locomotives entered the Soviet Union as reparations.
They returned to Germany until November 1952.
In the Reichsbahnbesserungswerk Dessau, the reappraisability of all six locomotives was determined.
[4] When an increasing demand for locomotives for the heavy freight train service arose as the reelectrification of the Central German network continued, the three locomotives E 95 01 to E 95 03 were worked up in 1959, with the other machines serving as spare parts dispensers.
The E 04, E 44 and E 94 present in larger quantities were first preferably constructed.
It was only when it was foreseeable that the number of reappraisals could not be further increased did the E 95 become the focus of reappraisal.
[4] Initially, the locomotives were stationed at the Leipzig-Wahren railway depot.
They came to the railway detectory in Halle P in 1960.
They were used in their own circulation plan mostly in front of coal trains from the Geiseltal in the direction of Halle, Leipzig and Erfurt.
With increasing age, the complicated control of the locomotives proved problematic.
So it often happened that shooters remained stuck during switching actions.
Then, the attendant with a hammer and wooden wedge, who was constantly driving on this locomotive, had to release the shooter again during the journey.
Damage to the traction motors occurred more often and led to months-long outages at the locomotive.
[5] Since there were enough new construction locomotives at the end of the 1960s and a significant expansion of the electrical operation was not planned at that time, the Reichsbahn was able to dispense with old locomotives and most of the splinter types, so that the E 95 were decommissioned.
In addition, it was concluded that the Deutsche Reichsbahn only wanted to occupy electric locomotives in one case, which would have resulted in a problem due to the aforementioned difficulties with the machines and the visibility of the driver's cab.
[5] In mid-1969, the E 95 02 and E 95 03 were the last to be replaced by the E 95 01, which terminated the service on 5 October 1970.
The proposed new designation 255 001 was therefore never used.
Except for the E 95 02, all locomotives were dismantled.
The E 95 02 has been erected as a replacement for the future museum locomotive E 77 10 as a turnout heating system at Halle Central Station.
It took some time before those responsible at the Reichsbahn could be persuaded that the E 95 was worth preserving as a museum locomotive.
In 1979, the initially external processing of the locomotive began with the aim of being able to present it for a vehicle show at the RAW Dessau.
[5] Since then it belonged to the Verkehrsmuseum Dresden as a nonoperable museum locomotive.
In 1986, the inner completion of the locomotive took place
In the DB Museum Halle (Saale) the locomotive museum is supervised.
Since 1995, employees of the traditional Bw Halle P have been working on the operational processing of the locomotive.
In April 2015, the locomotive could be presented for the first time for an exhibition in the Museums-Bw Arnstadt with new internal and external paintwork, whereby the transformers were checked and five traction motors are operational.
-The locomotives were used until 1945 on the Silesian mountain railway and the branching sections, where they proved themselves due to their reliable propulsion and low maintenance.
In February 1945, the machines were relocated to the central German network in Leipzig.
The main area of application was initially Silesia
