To invalidate fake news - Biontech CEO publishes vaccination photo
Uğur Şahin posted on the social network "LinkedIn" a photo of his first vaccination in early 2021
Biontech CEO Uğur Şahin had so far not publicly commented on his vaccination status.
Now the entrepreneur posted a photo showing his corona vaccine.
The illness of a friend had led him to do so, Şahin said.
The CEO and co-founder of the Mainz-based Biontech company, Uğur Şahin, has published a photo that shows he receiving a vaccination against the coronavirus.
There was misinformation on social media that I allegedly wasn't vaccinated.
In fact, my wife and I have received 3 doses of the Biontech-Pfizer vaccine," Şahin wrote Friday in a post on the "LinkedIn" network.
“For a few months, friends have been urging me to post a photo of me being vaccinated to counter these false rumors.”
So far, he has rejected this, because the communication should not revolve around himself.
"I was deeply saddened when I learned that an old school friend of mine is currently fighting for his life.
He's battling a severe Covid-19 condition and has been hanging on a ventilator for three weeks.
The school friend and his family were not vaccinated, Şahin wrote.
He didn't ask for the reasons.
“However, the most common cause seems to be anxiety, which is often stoked by misinformation and distrust.”
Fear is difficult to fight with facts, sometimes it is better to share personal experiences.
An interview circulates on the Internet with Deutsche Welle in which Uğur Şahin says he has not been vaccinated.
However, the video was created in December 2020, as Deutsche Welle itself clarified.
"I'm not legally allowed to get the vaccine yourself," Şahin said at the time.
There is a prioritization in the vaccination of the vaccine in Germany.
Meanwhile, prioritization has been lifted for a long time - and the Biontech boss has been vaccinated three times.
Fire at substation: Tens of thousands of people without electricity
In several municipalities in North Rhine-Westphalia, the lights went out on Friday evening: no more electricity and in many cases probably no heating.
A fire had paralyzed a substation.
After a fire with severe damage in a substation, tens of thousands of people in the Rhein-Sieg-Kreis in North Rhine-Westphalia had to do without electricity for about 24 hours.
Due to the blaze on Friday evening with 41,000 affected residents in several communities, the lights went out.
The utility Westnetz reported that numerous emergency power generators had been in operation on Saturday until the large-scale disturbance could finally be completely fixed in the late evening.
Firefighters and helpers of the THW provided support in the municipalities.
Authorities offered care places in schools and called for neighbourhood assistance.
The provider thanked those affected for their patience and the helpers for their commitment.
We are very aware that the last 24 hours have brought significant restrictions.
We would also like to thank the affected municipalities, the district, the fire brigades and the THW for the tireless commitment," Florian Witt, the head of the primary technology department, said in a message on Saturday evening.
The repair of the damage will take a few more days, as the West network fitters were initially able to recommissionally recommissurally, he explained.
According to the company, a technical defect led to the fire.
Electricity is flowing again
According to Westnetz, the fire, which broke out at around 19:15 o'clock on Friday, severely damaged parts of the 10,000-volt switch in the Neunkirchen-Seelscheid substation.
Extensive cleaning and repair work is necessary there.
In parallel, a connection to a 30,000-volt voltage level had been created.
With the help of this bypass, the power supply stands again.
Since Saturday 6 p.m., over 21,000 people in Much and Ruppichteroth would have had electricity again.
A further 20,000 inhabitants of Neunkirchen-Seelscheid had been gradually switched on from 21.30.
Since 23.15 o'clock, even those still affected by the failure would have electricity.
More than 80 installers from Westnetz and partner companies were deployed.
Authorities had also called for neighborhood assistance after setting up an emergency power supply for key buildings on Saturday noon.
"It is now important that the citizens in their neighborhood take care of the elderly or single people and communicate them to the local firefighting equipment houses when help is needed," Landrat Sebastian Schuster (CDU) explained after a crisis meeting.
During the set-up and operation of emergency power generators, the focus was on the residents of old people’s homes.
In addition, according to the authorities, temporary care places have been set up in schools for those people who could not take care of themselves or who need electricity-dependent medical devices or are dependent on medical care.
