In order to make the paint application even and clean, you should clean the components from dust, grease and fingerprints with some soapy water, rinse them with clear water and dry.
Before bringing the brush to the model for the first time with a new paint, it is recommended to practice 2 to 3 strokes.
You can do this on the interior of the components, which will no longer be visible later or on a yogurt cup.
The first line on the component
Pull the brush even next to the dash.
Slight overlapping of the paint layers later ensures a uniform area.
Avoid swiping back and forth quickly with the brush.
This creates unrealistically acting surfaces.
A model is not a garden fence.
Begin with bright colors.
The darks might otherwise shine through later.
Metal tones and metallic colours come at the very end with a separate brush.
Otherwise, unsightly metal streaks can occur if they mix with normal colors.
Let the painted parts dry for a day before installing them.
Faithful appearance reaches the models through the decals or decals.
From sovereign signs on airplanes to ship names to trimming strips and license plates in car models: the decals give the model the finishing touch.
Attach the decals in the correct order as long as the components are still easy to reach.
An aircraft cockpit can be glued more easily before it is installed in the hull.
Even more important than the right tools such as scissors, tweezers and toothpicks are time, patience and a steady hand.
Cut out each required decal individually and set it up.
Decals are also called water slide patterns, because you briefly moisten their backside with water until the carrier paper darkens due to the wet.
Then you push the picture only a small piece of the paper and place the piece on the appropriate place on the model (which you have moistened before).
While holding the decal on the model with a tool, you pull away the backing paper sideways.
Attention: Always check whether the picture fits properly before you push it completely off the paper so that it does not stick, wrinkle or even tear.
Suction excess water with a household towel.
