Fertility treatment at PKV / What is an "insurance case"?
hello together,
We have been in fertility treatment for some time, now when it comes to the billing with the PKV, it suddenly turns across.
In turn:
We had applied for the fertility train in advance at the PKV and got the commitment over 3 attempts.
The experiments were not restricted in any way (e.g. time frame, success, etc.).
The first attempt led directly to the successful pregnancy and birth and was billed with the PKV.
Some time later we tried to have a second child and carried out 2 (unsuccessful) attempts, but did not yet reckon with the PKV.
We again requested the assumption of costs at the PKV and were again granted 3 further attempts.
After a few further attempts (we have carried out more attempts than promised, logically calculate the most expensive with the PKV) we submitted the calculations to the PKV and got the nasty surprise: instead of 5 attempts (2+3), the PKV only wants to reimburse 3 attempts.
The justification: With pregnancy/birth, the remaining 2 attempts of the first commitment expire.
The second desire to have children is a new insurance case.
According to the terms and conditions, however, "The insurance claim begins with the healing treatment, it ends when treatment no longer exists."
The questions for the experts in the round here:
* Who is right, the PKV (only 3 attempts will be refunded) or we (there are 5 attempts to reimburse)?
* Would the PKV have had to point out to us in the commitments that the further attempts expire with a birth?
* Is the desire for a second child really a new insurance case?
Infertility (due to closed fallopian tubes) is not cured by the first child.
It'd be great if the experts would give your opinion here.
Thank you very much for your help!
