'Half victory' against Belarus linked with high cost to the EU
According to political expert Raphael Bossong, the EU can announce a "half victory" in the conflict with the Belarusian regime.
However, the costs are very high and meant massive violations of the rights of migrants and refugees to Belarus" border with Poland and Lithuania, Bossong told the Evangelical press service (epd).
The EU did not solve the confrontation sustainably, explained the researcher of the Berlin Foundation for Science and Politics (SWP).
This state of limbo - Belarus doesn't give quite small, but the EU feels as a winner - will probably continue for a while.
Dealing with the people at the border is not in line with the European Convention on Human Rights, Bossong ruled.
There is apparently an “increasing normalization of pushbacks” by individual EU governments.
Since the EU as a whole does not turn decisively against it, this is at the expense of fundamental rights.
At the same time, this development weakens cohesion in the Union.
"She apparently can't assert herself if a member stats in his minder to do his thing," such as Poland, which doesn't even allow the EU border protection agency Frontex access.
In addition, the EU pays a further price for its policy at the border with Belarus, according to Bossong: A basic agreement on asylum policy would be made more difficult, since the "border protection, border protection, border protection" line has been strengthened.
Renewed Attempt to Cross Borders
According to Poland's border guidances, migrants have once again tried to access Polish territory via Belarus.
55 migrants had been arrested during the night on Sunday and returned to the border, a spokeswoman for the authority said on Sunday.
The Polish officials had been pelted with stones by the migrants in the incident not far from the town of Czeremsza.
According to the information, a soldier was hit in the face and had to be medically treated by a military doctor.
In total, the border guard registered 52 attempted border crossings within 24 hours.
Already on Friday evening, a group of 28 migrants had overcome the border barriers.
Thousands of people from countries such as Iraq and Afghanistan had crossed or attempted the border between Belarus and the EU in recent months.
The EU accuses Alexander Lukashenko as a ruler in Minsk of attracting them with the prospect of entering the EU.
Poland and Lithuania, according to press reports, cordoned off border sections.
The EU sanctioned Belarus and threatened airlines bringing migrants and refugees into the country.
In addition, EU commissioners travelled to people’s countries of origin in order to prevent departures.
The most effective element of the EU's reaction is the pressure on the airlines, Bossong said.
"That's when the EU has thrown its market power into the balance and demonstrated its determination to operate with hard bandages and extraterritorial sanctions."
Minsk must also consider domestic political costs.
The Belarusian society is probably not prepared for the fact that the security apparatus regularly smuggles thousands of people through the country.
Mercedes protests after controversial Formula One final
Max Verstappen is said to have committed two rule breaks during the race, according to Mercedes.
Abu Dhabi - After Abu Dhabi's controversious Formula One final, Mercedes has appealed.
The Silver Arrows suspect two rule-breakings in the last Grand Prix of the year.
A protest refers to an alleged violation by Max Verstappen in the Red Bull in the last safety car phase, when he is said to have overtaken irregularly.
A second objection has been lodged against the ranking at the end of the race.
According to Mercedes view, the protocol was not followed in accordance with the rules.
Verstappen had overtaken Lewis Hamilton on the last lap in the Mercedes and had raced to his first Formula 1 title.
The manoeuvre took place shortly after the end of a safety-car phase, in which the Dutchman had overtaken cars in front of him.
