When it's time to change your checking account
Anyone who would have thought there would be quiet with the current accounts at first, is mistaken.
While many banks have used the autumn to let their customers confirm the fee increases of the past with all sorts of tricks, the allowances for negative interest rates continue to fall.
Deutsche Bank, for example, has long been considered relatively generous with a limit of 100,000 euros.
Now she has just halved the allowance and even quartered it for the daily allowance.
In a first step, the limit was lowered only for customers of the Postbank Group division - to €50,000 for checking and investment accounts and €25,000 for daily allowance accounts.
In the meantime, however, these lower allowances apply to the entire Deutsche Bank, as a spokesman confirmed on request.
And not only for new contracts, but also for "new individual agreements with existing customers".
Commerzbank had previously gone down to a €50,000 limit, which still applies, as with ING Deutschland.
DKB, which is particularly popular with cost-conscious customers, has lowered the allowances in two stages, initially to €50,000, and now to €25,000.
After all, more than 100 banks now have an allowance of 25,000 euros or less, according to figures from the Internet portal Verivox.
Sparda-Bank Berlin has also reached €25,000: However, a legal dispute with the consumer centres is still ongoing there, which in the first instance before the Regional Court of Berlin against this regulation.
It is now already being debated in the banks whether the institutions should possibly make provisions - in the event that even the upper authorities declare the negative interest rates inadmissible in principle and many millions must be repaid to bank customers.
