Kimi Räikkönen's departure from Formula 1 went even quieter than expected.
Abu Dhabi - The 42-year-old Finn took off the gloves and swiped the sweat from his forehead.
That's it.
After a driving error and a slight gang contact, Räikkönen had to park his Alfa Romeo at the Abu Dhabi Grand Price after not even half the race distance.
It was his 349th and last Grand Prix, the "Iceman" finishes his career in the motorsport premier class.
His wife Minttu and the two children had also traveled to the United Arab Emirates.
Räikkonen's greatest success was winning the 2007 World Cup in the Ferrari.
He scored 21 Grand Prix victories, started 18 times from the pole and reached the podium 103 times.
Frederich picks up 60th World Cup victory - Nolte wins in two-man bobsleigh - Sport
The brand of Norwegian biathlete Ole Einar Björndalen with 94 successes is the goal of the bobsleigh pilot.
"I once figured it out, I'd have to score nine and a half wins per season, then I could make it to Cortina 2026," said the double Olympic champion from BSC Oberbärenburg, who recorded two successes in two days at the races in Winterberg.
First, he fended Johannes Lochner's attack by five hundredths on Saturday, in which he raced from third to one.
Then he changed slightly to the material setting and drove out a bigger lead with 0.30 seconds on Sunday.
With his team Thorsten Margis (SV Halle), Candy Bauer (BSC Oberbärenburg) and Alexander Schüller (SV Halle) he prevailed in front of the Briton Brad Hall.
Austrian Benjamin Maier finished third.
"We took a greater risk today and were rewarded," said Friedrich, who also claimed his 22nd victory in series.
Berchtesgadener Johannes Lochner, who was driving for Stuttgart, came in fifth this time after finishing second earlier this time.
Christoph Hafer from BC Bad Feilnbach landed eighth after finishing fourth in the first race.
"If I knew what he's doing differently, I'd do it the same way," Lochner said.
Nolte with home win - podium for Jamanka
Laura Nolte scored the women's third win of the season in the two-man bobsleigh on her home track.
After it was only enough for the fourth place in the monobob the day before, she raced on Sunday in the German triple success with pusher Deborah Levi to the World Cup victory.
Two-bob Olympic champion Mariama Jamanka from BRC Thuringia came in second with Germany's fastest sprinter, Alexandra Burghardt from Burghausen.
In addition to her Olympic qualifier, she recorded the first podium finish this winter.
Wiesbaden's Kim Kalicki landed in third place with Leonie Fiebig.
For me it was super cool, first podium in the World Cup, I'm super happy that Mariama takes me.
I'm super happy with development.
There’s still a lot of potential at the start, but the starting times today were okay,” said Burghardt and emphasized: “It’s not just pushing, but there’s also a lot to do around it.”
Jamanka was a little annoyed by the not quite error-free rides, but said: "It's going in the right direction".
