Rotinger brake discs are designed for the demands of the dynamic driver, who appreciates reliability and safety during braking even in difficult conditions.
Roting brake discs are made of cast iron and then machined (drilled and cut).
All tuned discs are produced in short series according to individual technical characteristics, which are suitable for certain slices and patterns.
On the basis of the individual technical drawing, a special production program is created, which depends on the characteristics of the original assembly with the balancing of the disc.
The tuned discs produced in this way ensure high comfort and braking effectiveness.
Additionally created openings increase the effectiveness of the cooling of the disc, whereby the cuts improve the cleaning of the brake discs and brake pads.
Aesthetical properties of our tuned windows underline the character of every car with an attractive and striking appearance.
Coloma is the city where an unexpected event shaped the history of the West Coast.
In the winter of 1848, a man discovered glowing chunks in the water at the southern fork of the American River.
Although he tried to keep his discovery secret, the news quickly spread and triggered the so-called “California Gold Rush” of ‘49.
Thousands came from near and far, making Coloma one of the country's fastest-growing and thriving cities.
Areas were staked out, tents pitched, and saloons and hotels were created almost overnight.
Everyone wanted their share of the country's wealth.
“Coloma or Bust” (Coloma or bankrupt).
In this game you are a pioneer who seeks wealth in the West and wants to make a name for himself.
You're going to look for gold, recruit workers, gather horses and build businesses.
You’ll also get an opportunity to explore the surrounding rivers and adjoining areas.
You're not alone - the other pioneers seem to have the same idea!
You have to outperform your fellow pioneers in order not to go bust with the rest of them.
Coloma is a quick strategy game with many ways to victory.
You choose your actions simultaneously, have to budget with your resources and build a tableau, which “works” more and more for you (so-called “Engine Building”).
The buildings on your tableau allow you to become more efficient, receive additional actions, or apply rules in your favor.
With these buildings and the dynamic bridge tiles, you can develop your individual playing style using fine adjustments.
Coloma plays over a three-year period from 1848 (the year in which the gold was discovered in Coloma), over 1849 (the Year of the California Gold Rush (the year in which California attained official statehood).
One year in Coloma consists of five laps.
During each round, players will secretively and at the same time select a location on the schedule where they can perform actions.
After that, their selection is uncovered and they place their pioneers on the appropriate locations.
If a location has the most pioneers, it will receive a barrier!
This deactivates one half of the location - thereby reducing the possible actions there.
Then the players alternately carry out actions, such as gaininging resources, moving carts, setting up tents, searching through rivers, building bridges, opening shops and positioning revolver heroes.
When all actions are completed, the pioneers return.
At the beginning of each round, the advertiser turns to a new location and changes the available promotions.
This continues until the advertiser has moved five times, i.e. has concealed each location once and the year ends.
There's a shooting in which players have to defend Coloma from a raid by angry bandits.
If the players have contributed enough turret heroes and are numerically superior to the bandits, they will receive rewards.
However, if they fail, they receive lesser rewards or end in the cemetery.
The game ends after the third shooting, then the final scoring takes place.
The player with the most victory points wins!
