What you describe is referred to as *Awareness*, i.e. sensory perceptions under general anesthesia.
These occur with a frequency of about 1 to 1000.
It is most common to hearing sounds/voices, to see images less often, and most rarely to perceive pain.
For this topic relevant: Cesarean sections are operations that are associated with an increased rate of awareness, because to protect the child a very deep anesthesia is avoided.
Previous episodes of Awareness should be mentioned during anesthesia preludation.
Certain anesthesia procedures such as total intravenous anesthesia (TIVA) are associated with a slightly increased risk.
