Career in social work - as someone with a self-insecure personality disorder?
I have completed an apprenticeship in software development and have been trained for about a year now.
Unfortunately, however, I increasingly notice that this profession is not right for me, which is why I have been thinking about starting again and studying social work for a long time.
The problem: I have a self-insecuring (or anxiously avoiding) personality disorder (ÄVPS).
This roughly means I don't have self-esteem and are accordingly shy.
I don’t have a problem talking to people on a factual level, but it’s very difficult to have casual conversations and I’ve been told many times that I’ve seemed unapproachable (although I’ve already got better).
Can I become a social worker like this?
I don’t want to work as a street worker or in an open youth meeting place, but rather in addiction counseling or family help, for example.
However, I don’t know if the specific everyday work is really as I imagine, and it’s difficult to find something on the internet.
The ÄVPS is already leading to problems in my job (for me, emotionally) because it pulls me down every time there is something to complain about by my code ("already the simplest of things screwed") and it's hard for me more experienced colleagues to ask for help ("I should really know myself how to do this" / "I'll stop him with my stupid questions from work" / "he has to consider me really unnequivocated" etc.).
I will probably have the problems in every profession but I wanted to mention it at this point.
