Volcanic eruption on La Palma: arguably the longest in history
In the almost three months since the beginning of the eruption, the lava flows on the Canary Island have caused great damage.
There's an end in sight?
With a duration of 85 days, the volcanic eruption on La Palma has been the longest in the well-known history of the Spanish Canary Island since Sunday.
Until now, the eruption of the Tehuya volcano in 1585 with 84 days was considered the longest on the Atlantic island off the west coast of Africa.
There are no reliable data on previous outbreaks.
The activity of the still nameless volcano in the Cumbre Vieja has declined somewhat in the past few days, but volcanologists did not dare to predict when it could end.
The indicators indicate a slowdown.
The best news is that they are stable," the president of the islander, Mariano Hernández Zapata, told the state-owned TV channel RTVE.
Damage in the millions
In the nearly three months since the beginning of the eruption, the more than 1000-degree-hot lava has destroyed nearly 2900 residential homes and other buildings, RTVE reported.
Nearly 1200 hectares were covered by a meter-thick lava layer.
That's equivalent to nearly 1700 football pitches.
More than 7000 residents had to be evacuated, the damage is estimated at more than 900 million euros.
Two new headlands with a size of about 50 acres were formed off the coast by cold lava.
Banana cultivation, which is immensely important for the island, of which about half of the approximately 85,000 inhabitants lives directly or indirectly and which was in crisis even before the volcanic eruption, was particularly badly affected.
225 hectares of banana plantations were destroyed, further areas severely affected, wrote the newspaper "El País".
Christmas and gifts
Christmas is just around the corner and the children in particular will hardly be able to wait for the heavenly feast.
The many colorful gifts under the Christmas tree connected with the spiritual atmosphere and the crackling tension have something seductive about themselves.
Christmas is one of the few traditional occasions that still hold our increasingly divided society together.
This makes it all the more important to become aware of the Christmas tradition of gift-giving, so that the gesture of gift-giving, which according to the French sociologist and ethnologist Marcel Mauss was a peacemaking "social treaty of natural peoples", escapes the rigid routine and can be filled with life again in the context of this tradition.
How St. Nicholas Became the Christ Child
Strictly speaking, it was the Protestants, through which the Christmas days were closely linked to the gifting.
Previously, gifts were handed over in one way or another on St. Nicholas Day.
The background of this still popular custom today is that St. Nicholas, as Bishop of Myra, secretly threw three women gold in their window in one night in order to save them another life in sin.
From this unconventional way of gift-giving, the well-popular custom emerged in Germany to fill the boots especially of the children with gifts.
Protestants encountered this custom because they reject mediators between God and people as "saint worship."
For these reasons, they are defending themselves against the institution.
Martin Luther turned St. Nicholas into the Christ Child and thus founded a new line of tradition.
The focus of the gifting thus shifted to Christmas Eve.
However, since most Protestants no longer see such detailed questions so narrowly, it is upset that the custom of St. Nicholas is still an integral part of the pre-Christmas period, similar to St. St. Nicholas.
Today you will find everything your heart desires in boots outside the door and under the Christmas tree from socks to sought-after luxury watches.
Variety of traditions in Christian countries
Sources prove a tradition of gift-giving since the 13th century on the day of St. Nicholas.
With the Reformation, the day of gift-giving shifted to Christmas Eve.
In all Christian countries, however, a cheerful cuddly muddle prevails over the question of who brings the gifts to the children.
In Germany, it depends on the respective region, whether the Christ Child appears on Christmas Eve or Santa Claus, who in principle is a kind of St. Nicholas to whom some peculiarities have been assigned over time.
These include, for example, his white beard, which has little in common with the rather strict beardown of the historical figure, its sleigh, which is pulled by reindeers and the preference of the fireplace to gain access to the houses.
In the Netherlands, St. Nicholas remains the central figure on Christmas.
In Spain, the Holy Three Kings give the gifts, while in Italy it is the witch Befana, who is said to have shown them the way to the baby Jesus.
In Scandinavia at times mythical creatures bring the gifts, while in Russia it brought father Frost to some glory.
Here it's not reindeer, it's three horses pulling his sleigh as a troika.
In the Czech Republic, the motherland of the Reformation in the wake of the Hussite Wars, the Christ Child is responsible for handing over the gifts.
