The trade fears because of the 2G regulation around the Christmas business.
In most shops, only those who have been vaccinated or reheaved are allowed.
The trade considers this unnecessary and laments sales slumps.
He also has a possible solution ready.
According to the Handelsverband Deutschland (HDE), the 2G regulations are spoiling the important Christmas business for the retail sector.
In the case of transactions with articles of non-daily demand, sales slumps were to be complained, criticized HDE Managing Director Stefan Genth.
According to a recent HDE survey of 1100 retailers, more than 70 percent of companies looked negatively at the remaining days of the year.
Many would have already written off the Christmas business.
"What was actually supposed to be the positive culmination of the year is now often a hole without a ground," Genth said.
Traders with 2G access restrictions lost almost a third of their pre-crisis sales in the past week.
It runs well only in the food retail sector as well as in the online sector.
The association demanded consequences from the dramatic development.
Purchasing with mask, distance and hygiene concept is a safe matter, said the chief executive.
"2G while shopping doesn't take us any further in the fight against the pandemic," he stressed.
The current regulations detered customers and required a high level of personnel.
The ribbon scheme had to be approved nationwide, he demanded.
After that, each customer only had to be inspected for 2G (vaccinated or recovered) and would have access to all shops on that day with the ribbon received.
In addition, sample checks should be sufficient in the retail sector, as in public transport under the 3G rule (vaccinated, recovered or tested).
Deutsche Euroshop, which specializes in shopping centres, also spoke of a difficult Christmas business.
The frequencies were most recently excluding 2G at a good 75 percent of the pre-Corona level, the sales at about 90 percent.
These figures will now surely fall in the middle of the so important Christmas business," CEO Wilhelm Wellner told Wirtschaftswoche.
Due to the high level of personnel and administrative effort, an attempt is made for the centers to find solutions and get approved.
This includes the so-called ribbon solution, in which "visitors, for example, at a central point in the center, once provide their 2G evidence and then - equipped with a bracelet similar to as in a club hotel - can enter undisturbed all shops".
