After the release of our new high-end broadband B 100-6 OHM, it was only a matter of time before our SOLO series would get offspring again.
SOLO 15 is the name of the new offspring and is visually reminiscent of the great models with the B 200 - 6 OHM.
However, in this building proposal, a suitably designed foot is included directly in order to raise the loudspeaker to the correct listening position.
This has no influence on the acoustics and can therefore be adjusted in length according to the conditions at home.
Due to the principle, the SOLO 15 is very suitable for people in whom the maximum volume plays a rather minor role, but an exceptionally high level is to be achieved.
A 10 cm loudspeaker is simply not able to generate such large sound pressure levels almost unfiltered.
But that doesn't mean the SOLO 15 sounds weak.
On the contrary, she appears considerably adult and extremely impressive and accurate for her size.
Tonally, the SOLO 15 was tuned with a slight high-frequency rise.
This is generally customary in broadbands in order to optimize the excessively high frequencies greatly increasing bundling.
Exactly oriented to the listening place, the SOLO 15 impresses with very clear and fine sound.
Slightly rotated outwards, the high-frequency range can be reduced and the sound image can be made warmer.
Thanks to the dot sound source, locating the individual components of a piece of music in the optimal stereo triangle is razor-sharp and amazingly precise.
The reference-suspicious extremely low mechanical losses of the B 100 - 6 OHM ensure a fine dynamic that of its same.
The SOLO 15 not only complements our SOLO series, but also expands it in terms of resolution and accuracy.
As with all our kits, it is advisable at the beginning to think about the final optics of the loudspeaker in order to include corresponding changes in the construction.
If, for example, you want to veneer the housing, the lowering of the loudspeaker must be adapted accordingly, since the veneer again applies material.
After all plates have been cut (e.g. in the hardware store), you should first drill an 8 mm hole into the base plate at the appropriate location in order to be able to carry out the cable later.
With the help of a router, the loudspeaker opening in the front now follows, so that the B 100 - 6 OHM can later be used flush.
On the rear side, this opening should be provided with a 45° chamfer in order to make the opening surface of the loudspeaker chassis as large as possible.
The four fitting points of the B 100 - 6 OHM get an interruption in the bevel, as showned in the “Detail Bevel” picture.
Afterwards you start with the actual construction of the box and place one of the two side walls on a flat surface.
On these, the rear wall, the lid, the front and the bottom are now glued.
The three stiffeners should also be used and glued.
The stiffeners themselves can vary in the material thickness if necessary, provided, for example, you want to resort to finished strips from the DIY store instead of producing them yourself from 16 mm MDF.
In the next work step, the second side is attached.
This is followed by all the plates of the foot, i.e. the two sides, as well as the front.
By means of a suitable hole circular saw or a forester drill, it is facilitated to attach the openings of the bass reflex tube, as well as the connection terminal on the rear wall.
Afterwards you use a rotary milling cutter to round off all the edges of the actual box.
The top side of the foot plate can also be rounded.
Subsequently, the foot plate is firmly bonded to the base of the housing and additionally screwed with long wooden screws.
This is followed by marking and pre-drilling (2.5 mm drilers) of the fitting points of the loudspeaker chassis and the connection terminal.
After the housing has been cleaned (suction/blowish out), you start laying the cable.
The easiest way to do this is through the 35 mm bass reflex opening in the rear wall.
The entire and unabridged cable is pushed piece by piece through the 8 mm bore into the foot up to the terminal opening and lets it protrude about 30 cm there.
In the upper part of the housing, the cable is guided to the loudspeaker opening and it is also allowed to hang loosely there.
Subsequently, the crossover is prepared and used.
The construction plan of the crossover, as well as the corresponding connection plan, can be taken from the following document: Assembly instructions Soft Solo 15.
If the crossover is installed and the connection terminal is screwed together, the 8 mm cable opening in the interior of the box follows, which has not yet been completely closed.
Thanks to the 35 mm bASSREFLEX opening and with a little skill, you can have hot glue dripped onto the opening and thus closed.
Due to the opening of the B 100 - 6 OHM and with a small lamp, you can check the seal accordingly.
Now the damping follows according to the description.
Subsequently, the B 100-6 OHM is connected and screwed with the corresponding screws.
The polarity plays an important role here, so that the positive line (marked cable) has to be soldered at the positive pole of the loudspeaker (larger contact).
Finally, the bass reflex tube is shortened to the specified length of 28 mm (total length of the tube) and inserted.
For damping, a mat of the damping material is cut into two parts and distributed loosely in the housing.
The opening around the bass reflex channel should be kept free.
